<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gobir</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1808 CE (approx.) · capital Alkalawa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Warlike northern Hausa kingdom whose clash with the scholar Usman dan Fodio set off the jihad that created the Sokoto Caliphate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa Bakwai</a>, is believed to have formed further north, near the Air massif, and moved south under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> pressure. Its rulers were known as the frontier guards of Hausaland. By the 15th century it occupied the lands of present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> was a military state relying on cavalry. In 1762 its king Babari sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-zamfara">Birnin Zamfara</a> and moved Gobir's capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>. Its rulers were Muslim but tolerated older practices, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, once tutor at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bawa-jan-gwarzo">Bawa Jan Gwarzo</a>, condemned in his preaching.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Sarkin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a> moved against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">dan Fodio's</a> community in 1804, the scholar declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a> fell in 1808 and Yunfa was killed. Gobir loyalists withdrew north and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-tsibiri">Sultanate of Tsibiri</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> took over the old Gobir lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1450 CE – 1808 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:7.12%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1808 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alkalawa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Babari, Bawa Jan Gwarzo, Yunfa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a href="/countries/niger">Niger</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gobir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1629&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gobir" aria-label="Kingdom of Gobir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4852 1671 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Gobir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5172 1927L5144 1947M5172 1927L5144 1947M5172 1927L5174 1951"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5144 1947h0M5144 1947h0M5174 1951h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5172 1927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5172 1927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Alkalawa. <a href="/atlas?year=1629&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gobir">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
